{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"6319f9a9dd7ead0c","name":"card_event_us_weather_bureau","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/OceaniaStorm/events/card_event_us_weather_bureau","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"US WEATHER BUREAU","text":"Choose One - Forecast OR draw a card.","flavorText":"The US Weather Bureau became an unexpected part of wartime security after the US entered WWII. Weather reports could help ships, aircraft and submarines plan movements, so the Office of Censorship restricted what newspapers and radio stations could share. Forecasts were shortened, weather maps disappeared, and broadcasters often needed permission before mentioning storms. This caused serious problems on the home front. On 16 March 1942, a powerful tornado outbreak struck the central and southern US. Near Memphis, radio station WREC knew doctors and nurses were needed in damaged communities, but censorship rules prevented it from giving clear public details until permission arrived from Washington hours later. A similar danger appeared in 1943, when a hurricane hit the Texas coast with little warning. Ships that normally sent weather reports from the Gulf of Mexico had stayed radio silent because of U-boats fear. The restrictions were intended to protect military operations, but they also weakened public trust in official forecasts. In October 1943, many limits on weather news were lifted after officials decided the cost to farming, aviation, shipping and public safety had become too high.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["ability.forecast"],"Name":"card_event_us_weather_bureau","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_us_weather_bureau_title","SourceString":"US WEATHER BUREAU","LocalizedString":"US WEATHER BUREAU"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_us_weather_bureau_text","SourceString":"Choose One - Forecast OR draw a card.","LocalizedString":"Choose One - Forecast OR draw a card."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_us_weather_bureau_history","SourceString":"The US Weather Bureau became an unexpected part of wartime security after the US entered WWII. Weather reports could help ships, aircraft and submarines plan movements, so the Office of Censorship restricted what newspapers and radio stations could share. Forecasts were shortened, weather maps disappeared, and broadcasters often needed permission before mentioning storms. This caused serious problems on the home front. On 16 March 1942, a powerful tornado outbreak struck the central and southern US. Near Memphis, radio station WREC knew doctors and nurses were needed in damaged communities, but censorship rules prevented it from giving clear public details until permission arrived from Washington hours later. A similar danger appeared in 1943, when a hurricane hit the Texas coast with little warning. Ships that normally sent weather reports from the Gulf of Mexico had stayed radio silent because of U-boats fear. The restrictions were intended to protect military operations, but they also weakened public trust in official forecasts. In October 1943, many limits on weather news were lifted after officials decided the cost to farming, aviation, shipping and public safety had become too high.","LocalizedString":"The US Weather Bureau became an unexpected part of wartime security after the US entered WWII. Weather reports could help ships, aircraft and submarines plan movements, so the Office of Censorship restricted what newspapers and radio stations could share. Forecasts were shortened, weather maps disappeared, and broadcasters often needed permission before mentioning storms. This caused serious problems on the home front. On 16 March 1942, a powerful tornado outbreak struck the central and southern US. Near Memphis, radio station WREC knew doctors and nurses were needed in damaged communities, but censorship rules prevented it from giving clear public details until permission arrived from Washington hours later. A similar danger appeared in 1943, when a hurricane hit the Texas coast with little warning. Ships that normally sent weather reports from the Gulf of Mexico had stayed radio silent because of U-boats fear. The restrictions were intended to protect military operations, but they also weakened public trust in official forecasts. In October 1943, many limits on weather news were lifted after officials decided the cost to farming, aviation, shipping and public safety had become too high."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","chooseOneCards":[{"cardText":{"Namespace":"","Key":"35AD3816419D4BA7D85A7AAE8C21C772","SourceString":"Forecast.","LocalizedString":"Forecast."},"selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":false},{"cardText":{"Namespace":"","Key":"8E795F544B533D862FBA1F9078A10D45","SourceString":"Draw a card.","LocalizedString":"Draw a card."},"selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":false}],"usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"spawnCardName":"card_event_sunny1_blue_sky;card_event_rain1_mist;card_event_storm1_gale","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/US_WEATHER_BUREAU.US_WEATHER_BUREAU","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_us_weather_bureau_photo","SourceString":"The US Weather Bureau station at the National Airport, Washington DC, 1943.","LocalizedString":"The US Weather Bureau station at the National Airport, Washington DC, 1943."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"isAttackBuff":true,"isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_us_weather_bureau","owner":"card_event_us_weather_bureau_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direc